e not hearing  because these are the days when you can  go online and you can find it and there  are a lot of great indigenous playlists  out there i've made a few myself  well so what's been your experience  because you know you're talking about  the the fact that yeah there's a little  bit of a um  not really recognition of the indigenous  music industry as opposed to other  industries that exist um  what's been your experience like growing  up and especially where before the the  digital age um  get being in this industry that has been  a little bit less diverse than it should  be and that's you know  [Laughter]  you are diplomatic  um  well it's been real hard i mean  it's been real hard to me  when i was growing up there weren't any  role models out there except for maybe  harry smith  uh from six nations uh also known as jay  hill rece j silverheels who created and  played the role of tonto on radio  television movies um and he was a friend  of mine eventually but when i was  growing up he was about the only  indigenous person that i would hear  about things are better now now that now  especially in canada but not so much in  the us you can pick up the newspaper and  read about indigenous people doing all  kinds of things everything you think of  um but we're absent in most of the rest  of the world we are just plain not there  uh just like we don't have a music  industry it's not it's not as though  anybody's trying to keep us out is  nobody's helping us to get in the junos  have done  they have they've done  about they've done some things you know  that are really significant but still  we're not where we need to be and the  you know i used to think that you know  i'd look back on my growing up listening  mostly to whatever was on the road you  know frank sinatra and tony bennett and  other um  singers of the time and then when black  music all of a sudden you can hear it on  the radio oh wow the combination was i  thought really exciting and when we  first established the music of  aboriginal canada category the journals  you know years ago um  i i just envisioned that  indigenous music along with everybody  else's music presented in the right way  could be a thing that people would enjoy  and we sure enjoy making the music so um  things are better now and are going to  continue to get better i think there are  lots of indigenous musicians out there  but it was very hard  and when i say there were no role models  i'm not kidding there certainly weren't  any in in music and i remember in the  60s i was invited to take a part in  television's first two-hour music for  television series called the virginian  and i said i would only take the part if  all of the indigenous  roles were played by indigenous people  and of course they said oh no that's  that's you know you're asking too much  and besides we have makeup artists and  we have they can turn a dog into a cat  and we have uh filipinos and italians  and jewish people we've got all kinds of  people who can play those roles but what  i said was the same thing is what i'll  say to you today  it's about more than just fooling white  people so that you can make a buck  it's about more than that we are real  we're your neighbors we're your friends  we're your relatives and we have  something to contribute that you don't  even know what you're missing  a lot of people are watching reservation  dogs that's good you know so there are  there is like um it gives you a hint  about the car the cross current of uh  indigenous talent that's there and it's  starting to develop and now at least you  can see you can see that one and  hopefully what it's going to do is make  you curious enough to poke around on the  internet and find us all because we are  here  and so i think you may have kind of  answered my next question on that a  little bit but you know what advice  would you give i think to non-indigenous  people who want to be allies  you know i'm on the board of the donny  we